# CAMS results summary

Every number in this file was read from a file in `results/` when it was built (`python -m scripts.make_report_assets`). The same numbers are listed in `docs/numbers_audit.csv`, and `python -m scripts.check_numbers` fails if any of them no longer matches its results file. Each section ends with the report chapter it belongs in. The results are frozen: no experiment was re-run for the report. The tables and figures mentioned are in `docs/report_assets/` (see `INDEX.md` there for the numbering).

In every table, a {{ lit('95%') }} interval is a bootstrap over patients on the locked holdout unless the text says otherwise. The positive class is always a no-show.

## Experiments on the prediction model

### E1 Dataset profile and cleaning

The Kaggle file holds {{ ds('kaggle', 'rows_raw') }} appointments. Cleaning removed {{ ds('kaggle', 'rows_removed') }} of them (the cleaning log counts each rule: {{ q('cleaning_log', {'dataset': 'kaggle', 'step': '3'}, 'rows_changed', 'int') }} fractional patient IDs, {{ q('cleaning_log', {'dataset': 'kaggle', 'step': '5'}, 'rows_changed', 'int') }} impossible ages, {{ q('cleaning_log', {'dataset': 'kaggle', 'step': '6'}, 'rows_changed', 'int') }} appointments dated before they were booked), leaving {{ ds('kaggle', 'rows_clean') }} appointments from {{ ds('kaggle', 'unique_patients_clean') }} patients between {{ ds('kaggle', 'appointment_date_min', 'str') }} and {{ ds('kaggle', 'appointment_date_max', 'str') }}. {{ ds('kaggle', 'no_show_rate_clean', 'pct') }} were no-shows. {{ ds('kaggle', 'share_same_day_booking', 'pct') }} of appointments are booked for the same day and only {{ eda('kaggle', '0 (same day)') }} of those are missed, against {{ eda('kaggle', '15-30') }} for bookings made {{ lit('15') }} to {{ lit('30') }} days ahead. The OpenML file has {{ ds('openml', 'rows_raw') }} appointments, {{ ds('openml', 'rows_clean') }} after removing exact duplicates, with {{ ds('openml', 'no_show_rate_clean', 'pct') }} no-shows. The Hangu file has {{ ds('hangu', 'rows_raw') }} consultations from {{ ds('hangu', 'sessions') }} half-day sessions, with a median of {{ ds('hangu', 'service_minutes_median', 1) }} minutes and a mean of {{ ds('hangu', 'service_minutes_mean', 1) }} minutes. The Kaggle holdout is the latest {{ ds('kaggle', 'n_holdout_dates', 0) }} of {{ ds('kaggle', 'n_appointment_dates', 0) }} appointment dates ({{ ds('kaggle', 'n_holdout_rows') }} appointments); OpenML holds out its last month. No patient appears on both sides of any cross-validation fold ({{ ds('kaggle', 'cv_patients_in_both_sides', 0) }} patients on both sides).

Report chapter: Chapter 6 (data design) and Chapter 8 (Results: Tables 1 and 2, Figures 1 to 4).

### E2 Baselines

Always predicting "show" looks good on accuracy ({{ q('e2_baselines', {'dataset': 'kaggle', 'model': 'always_show'}, 'accuracy') }} on the Kaggle holdout) but is a coin flip on AUC-ROC ({{ q('e2_baselines', {'dataset': 'kaggle', 'model': 'always_show'}, 'auc_roc') }}), and its average precision is just the no-show rate ({{ q('e2_baselines', {'dataset': 'kaggle', 'model': 'always_show'}, 'avg_precision') }}). That is why accuracy is never the headline. A rule that uses only a patient's earlier no-show rate reaches an AUC-ROC of {{ qci('e2_baselines', {'dataset': 'kaggle', 'model': 'earlier_no_show_rate_rule'}, 'auc_roc') }}: little signal there, partly because patients average only {{ ds('kaggle', 'mean_appointments_per_patient', 2) }} appointments in this file.

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Table 3).

### E3 Model comparison on the Kaggle data

With every feature the dataset offers, the holdout AUC-ROC is {{ e3ci('logistic_regression') }} for logistic regression, {{ e3ci('random_forest') }} for the random forest and {{ e3ci('hist_gradient_boosting') }} for gradient boosting. All three are well below the {{ lit('0.85') }} level that would signal leakage, and a model trained on shuffled labels scores {{ e3('control_shuffled_labels') }}, as it should. Moving from the earlier-rate rule to logistic regression and then to the random forest are clear improvements, with changes in AUC-ROC of {{ qci('e3_ladder', {'step': 'earlier_no_show_rate_rule -> logistic_regression'}, 'delta_auc_roc', 's3') }} and {{ qci('e3_ladder', {'step': 'logistic_regression -> random_forest'}, 'delta_auc_roc', 's3') }}. Gradient boosting over the random forest is not clearly better: the change is {{ qci('e3_ladder', {'step': 'random_forest -> hist_gradient_boosting'}, 'delta_auc_roc', 's3') }}, so the two are statistically close. Brier scores are {{ e3('random_forest', 'brier') }} for the random forest and {{ e3('hist_gradient_boosting', 'brier') }} for gradient boosting; the logistic regression's {{ e3('logistic_regression', 'brier') }} is not comparable because it uses class weights. At {{ lit('40%') }} precision gradient boosting finds {{ e3('hist_gradient_boosting', 'sens_at_prec_40', 3) }} of the no-shows, and at {{ lit('50%') }} precision {{ e3('hist_gradient_boosting', 'sens_at_prec_50', 3) }}: the model ranks patients usefully but cannot be confident about many of them.
{% set _ = expect(v('e3_ladder', {'step': 'random_forest -> hist_gradient_boosting'}, 'delta_auc_roc_lo') < 0 < v('e3_ladder', {'step': 'random_forest -> hist_gradient_boosting'}, 'delta_auc_roc_hi'), 'gradient boosting over the random forest is not clearly better') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e3_ladder', {'step': 'logistic_regression -> random_forest'}, 'delta_auc_roc_lo') > 0, 'random forest clearly beats logistic regression') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e3_kaggle_models', {'model': 'hist_gradient_boosting'}, 'auc_roc_hi') < 0.85, 'AUC stays under the leakage alarm') %}

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Table 4, Table 5, Figures 5 and 6).

### E4 Which features matter

With patient-grouped cross-validation on the training part, the reference model with all features has an AUC-ROC of {{ q('e4_ablation', {'variant': 'all_features'}, 'cv_auc_mean') }}. Removing lead time and weekday changes the AUC-ROC the most, by {{ q('e4_ablation', {'variant': 'drop_lead_calendar'}, 'delta_auc_vs_all', 's3') }}, and those two alone keep an AUC-ROC of {{ q('e4_ablation', {'variant': 'only_lead_calendar'}, 'cv_auc_mean') }}. Removing age and sex changes it by {{ q('e4_ablation', {'variant': 'drop_demographics'}, 'delta_auc_vs_all', 's3') }}, removing patient history by {{ q('e4_ablation', {'variant': 'drop_history'}, 'delta_auc_vs_all', 's3') }} and removing the SMS reminder by {{ q('e4_ablation', {'variant': 'no_sms_received'}, 'delta_auc_vs_all', 's3') }}; history alone reaches only {{ q('e4_ablation', {'variant': 'only_history'}, 'cv_auc_mean') }}. The SMS reminder needs care: it was sent selectively, to {{ q('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '1-3', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'share_of_band_with_sms', 'pct') }} of bookings made one to three days ahead but {{ q('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '4-7', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'share_of_band_with_sms', 'pct') }} of those made four to seven days ahead. Within every lead-time band that has both groups, patients who received an SMS missed less: the no-show rate was {{ q('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '1-3', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'rate_difference_sms_minus_none', 'pts') }} percentage points different (SMS minus none) in the shortest band and {{ q('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '31+', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'rate_difference_sms_minus_none', 'pts') }} in the longest. That is an association, not proof that the reminder causes it.
{% set _ = expect(v('e4_ablation', {'variant': 'drop_lead_calendar'}, 'delta_auc_vs_all') < v('e4_ablation', {'variant': 'drop_demographics'}, 'delta_auc_vs_all') < 0, 'lead time and weekday matter most') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '1-3', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'rate_difference_sms_minus_none') < 0 and v('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '4-7', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'rate_difference_sms_minus_none') < 0 and v('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '8-14', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'rate_difference_sms_minus_none') < 0 and v('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '15-30', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'rate_difference_sms_minus_none') < 0 and v('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '31+', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'rate_difference_sms_minus_none') < 0, 'the SMS group misses less in every band that has one') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '1-3', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'rate_difference_sms_minus_none') > v('e4_sms_crosstab', {'lead_time_band': '31+', 'sms_received': '1'}, 'rate_difference_sms_minus_none'), 'the difference grows with lead time') %}

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Tables 6 and 7, Figure 7).

### E5 The same ladder on OpenML

On the OpenML holdout (its last month) the AUC-ROC is {{ e5ci('logistic_regression') }} for logistic regression, {{ e5ci('random_forest') }} for the random forest and {{ e5ci('hist_gradient_boosting') }} for gradient boosting, so the random forest and gradient boosting are statistically close here too, and both are clearly above logistic regression. These scores are lower than on Kaggle. The OpenML file does not say where it comes from and has no patient ID, so it cannot say why; it is a second dataset that shows the same ordering of models, not a replication of the Kaggle numbers.

{% set _ = expect(v('e5_openml_models', {'model': 'random_forest'}, 'auc_roc_lo') > v('e5_openml_models', {'model': 'logistic_regression'}, 'auc_roc_hi'), 'on OpenML the random forest is clearly above logistic regression') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e5_openml_models', {'model': 'hist_gradient_boosting'}, 'auc_roc_hi') < v('e3_kaggle_models', {'model': 'hist_gradient_boosting'}, 'auc_roc_lo'), 'OpenML scores are lower than Kaggle scores') %}
Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Table 8).

### E6 Does it transfer?

Using only the four features both datasets share (age, sex, lead time, weekday), a model trained on Kaggle scores {{ e6('kaggle_to_openml', 'logistic_regression', 'auc_transfer') }} (logistic regression) and {{ e6('kaggle_to_openml', 'hist_gradient_boosting', 'auc_transfer') }} (gradient boosting) on OpenML, against {{ e6('kaggle_to_openml', 'logistic_regression', 'auc_within_target') }} and {{ e6('kaggle_to_openml', 'hist_gradient_boosting', 'auc_within_target') }} for models trained on OpenML itself. The AUC-ROC drops (positive means the transferred model is worse) are {{ e6ci('kaggle_to_openml', 'logistic_regression', 'auc_drop', 's3') }} and {{ e6ci('kaggle_to_openml', 'hist_gradient_boosting', 'auc_drop', 's3') }}; the first interval includes zero. In the other direction a model trained on OpenML scores {{ e6('openml_to_kaggle', 'logistic_regression', 'auc_transfer') }} and {{ e6('openml_to_kaggle', 'hist_gradient_boosting', 'auc_transfer') }} on the Kaggle holdout, against {{ e6('openml_to_kaggle', 'logistic_regression', 'auc_within_target') }} and {{ e6('openml_to_kaggle', 'hist_gradient_boosting', 'auc_within_target') }}: drops of {{ e6ci('openml_to_kaggle', 'logistic_regression', 'auc_drop', 's3') }} and {{ e6ci('openml_to_kaggle', 'hist_gradient_boosting', 'auc_drop', 's3') }}. The findings transfer only partly: the main Kaggle signal, lead time, behaves differently in the two datasets (same-day bookings are missed {{ eda('kaggle', '0 (same day)') }} of the time on Kaggle but {{ eda('openml', '0 (same day)') }} on OpenML), which is a plausible reason but one these data cannot confirm.
{% set _ = expect(v('e6_transfer', {'direction': 'kaggle_to_openml', 'model': 'logistic_regression'}, 'auc_drop_lo') < 0 < v('e6_transfer', {'direction': 'kaggle_to_openml', 'model': 'logistic_regression'}, 'auc_drop_hi'), 'the logistic-regression drop from Kaggle to OpenML includes zero') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e6_transfer', {'direction': 'openml_to_kaggle', 'model': 'logistic_regression'}, 'auc_drop_lo') > 0 and v('e6_transfer', {'direction': 'openml_to_kaggle', 'model': 'hist_gradient_boosting'}, 'auc_drop_lo') > 0, 'transfer from OpenML to Kaggle loses AUC') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e6_transfer', {'direction': 'kaggle_to_openml', 'model': 'hist_gradient_boosting'}, 'auc_drop_lo') > 0, 'transfer from Kaggle to OpenML loses AUC for gradient boosting') %}

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Table 9, Figure 8).

### E7 Imbalance handling

Class weights and threshold tuning leave the ranking (AUC-ROC) unchanged: for logistic regression it is {{ e7('logistic_regression', 'none', 'auc_roc') }} without class weights and {{ e7('logistic_regression', 'class_weights', 'auc_roc') }} with them. Class weights do distort the probabilities, though: the mean predicted risk rises to {{ e7('logistic_regression', 'class_weights', 'mean_predicted') }} against a true rate of {{ e7('logistic_regression', 'none', 'prevalence') }}, and the Brier score from {{ e7('logistic_regression', 'none', 'brier') }} to {{ e7('logistic_regression', 'class_weights', 'brier') }}. Resampling is worse. SMOTE lowers gradient boosting's AUC-ROC to {{ e7('hist_gradient_boosting', 'smote', 'auc_roc') }} and raises its Brier score to {{ e7('hist_gradient_boosting', 'smote', 'brier') }}; NearMiss collapses the AUC-ROC to {{ e7('logistic_regression', 'nearmiss', 'auc_roc') }} (logistic regression) and {{ e7('hist_gradient_boosting', 'nearmiss', 'auc_roc') }} (gradient boosting). The final model therefore uses no resampling; it stays in the report only as an ablation.
{% set _ = expect(v('e7_imbalance', {'base_model': 'hist_gradient_boosting', 'strategy': 'smote'}, 'auc_roc') < v('e7_imbalance', {'base_model': 'hist_gradient_boosting', 'strategy': 'none'}, 'auc_roc'), 'SMOTE lowers AUC') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e7_imbalance', {'base_model': 'logistic_regression', 'strategy': 'class_weights'}, 'brier') > v('e7_imbalance', {'base_model': 'logistic_regression', 'strategy': 'none'}, 'brier'), 'class weights worsen Brier') %}

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Table 10).

### E8 Calibration

On patient-grouped cross-validation the Brier score of gradient boosting is {{ e8('uncalibrated', 'brier_cv') }} with no calibration, {{ e8('sigmoid', 'brier_cv') }} with Platt scaling and {{ e8('isotonic', 'brier_cv') }} with isotonic regression: a tie to four decimals. Because neither calibrator lowers the Brier score, the selection rule keeps the model uncalibrated, and the model used later (in the deployable model, the simulation and the app) is uncalibrated. On the holdout its expected calibration error is {{ q('e8_calibration', {'stage': 'holdout', 'method': 'uncalibrated'}, 'ece', 4) }} and its Brier score {{ qci('e8_calibration', {'stage': 'holdout', 'method': 'uncalibrated'}, 'brier', 4) }}. The reliability diagram sits close to the diagonal, with the highest-risk bin slightly over-predicting (predicted {{ q('e8_reliability', {'version': 'uncalibrated', 'bin': '10'}, 'mean_predicted', 'pct') }}, observed {{ q('e8_reliability', {'version': 'uncalibrated', 'bin': '10'}, 'observed_rate', 'pct') }}).
{% set _ = expect((v('e8_calibration', {'stage': 'grouped_cv', 'method': 'sigmoid'}, 'brier_cv') - v('e8_calibration', {'stage': 'grouped_cv', 'method': 'uncalibrated'}, 'brier_cv'))|abs < 0.00005, 'Platt scaling ties with no calibration') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e8_calibration', {'stage': 'grouped_cv', 'method': 'uncalibrated'}, 'brier_cv') <= v('e8_calibration', {'stage': 'grouped_cv', 'method': 'isotonic'}, 'brier_cv'), 'isotonic does not improve Brier') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e8_reliability', {'version': 'uncalibrated', 'bin': '10'}, 'mean_predicted') > v('e8_reliability', {'version': 'uncalibrated', 'bin': '10'}, 'observed_rate'), 'the top bin over-predicts') %}

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Table 11, Figure 9).

### E9 The deployable model

The model the app and the simulation use sees only what a clinic can collect: age, sex, lead time, weekday, earlier appointments and earlier no-show rate. On the holdout it reaches an AUC-ROC of {{ e9ci('deployable', 'auc_roc') }}, average precision {{ e9ci('deployable', 'avg_precision') }} and Brier score {{ e9ci('deployable', 'brier') }}, against {{ e9ci('research_all_features', 'auc_roc') }} for the research model with every feature. Giving up the features the app cannot collect changes the AUC-ROC by {{ e9ci('deployable', 'delta_auc_roc_vs_research', 's3') }}: small, but the interval excludes zero. The loss shows more at high precision: sensitivity at {{ lit('40%') }} precision is {{ e9ci('deployable', 'sens_at_prec_40') }} against {{ e9ci('research_all_features', 'sens_at_prec_40') }}. The model is uncalibrated (see E8), was trained on Kaggle data only, and uses age and sex.
{% set _ = expect(v('e9_deployable', {'stage': 'holdout', 'model': 'deployable'}, 'delta_auc_roc_vs_research_hi') < 0, 'the deployable model is slightly worse than the research model') %}

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Table 12) and Chapter 6 (the model that the app uses).

### E10 Risk thresholds and bands

The model card sets the Medium cut at {{ e10('chosen', 'medium', 'threshold', 2) }} and the High cut at {{ e10('chosen', 'high', 'threshold', 2) }}. On the holdout the bands are: Low, {{ e10('band', 'low', 'share_flagged') }} of bookings, of which {{ e10('band', 'low', 'precision') }} are no-shows; Medium, {{ e10('band', 'medium', 'share_flagged') }} of bookings and {{ e10('band', 'medium', 'precision') }} no-shows; High, {{ e10('band', 'high', 'share_flagged') }} of bookings and {{ e10('band', 'high', 'precision') }} no-shows. The cuts were chosen on out-of-fold predictions for the training part, aiming at a precision near {{ lit('30%') }} for Medium and {{ lit('40%') }} for High; on the holdout they reach {{ e10('chosen', 'medium', 'precision') }} and {{ e10('chosen', 'high', 'precision') }}, a little short of those aims. The High band holds {{ e10('band', 'high', 'sensitivity') }} of all no-shows, and Medium and High together hold {{ e10('chosen', 'medium', 'sensitivity') }}. So a High flag roughly doubles the chance of a no-show compared with the clinic average ({{ ds('kaggle', 'holdout_no_show_rate', 'pct') }} on the holdout), and a Low flag cuts it to about a third, but even High bookings attend more often than not. That is why the flag is advisory.
{% set _ = expect(v('e10_thresholds', {'kind': 'band', 'label': 'high'}, 'precision') < 0.5, 'High-risk bookings still attend more often than not') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e10_thresholds', {'kind': 'chosen', 'label': 'medium'}, 'precision') < 0.30 and v('e10_thresholds', {'kind': 'chosen', 'label': 'high'}, 'precision') < 0.40, 'the holdout precision at the chosen cuts is a little below the aims') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e10_thresholds', {'kind': 'band', 'label': 'low'}, 'precision') < v('e10_thresholds', {'kind': 'band', 'label': 'medium'}, 'precision') < v('e10_thresholds', {'kind': 'band', 'label': 'high'}, 'precision'), 'the bands are ordered') %}
{% set _ = expect(1.8 < v('e10_thresholds', {'kind': 'band', 'label': 'high'}, 'precision') / v('dataset_summary', {'dataset': 'kaggle', 'metric': 'holdout_no_show_rate'}, 'value') < 2.2, 'a High flag roughly doubles the chance of a no-show') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('e10_thresholds', {'kind': 'band', 'label': 'low'}, 'precision') / v('dataset_summary', {'dataset': 'kaggle', 'metric': 'holdout_no_show_rate'}, 'value') < 0.4, 'a Low flag cuts the chance of a no-show to about a third') %}

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Tables 13 and 14, Figure 10) and Chapter 6 (the risk flag).

## Experiments on the booking simulation

### S1 Sanity tests and the consultation-time fit

The simulation was checked before it was used: {{ q('test_summary', {'group': 'module', 'name': 'tests/test_sim.py'}, 'passed', 'int') }} tests in `tests/test_sim.py` and {{ q('test_summary', {'group': 'module', 'name': 'tests/test_sim_experiments.py'}, 'passed', 'int') }} in `tests/test_sim_experiments.py` pass. They cover the cases the plan lists (everyone attends and service equals the slot: no wait and no overtime; nobody attends: nobody served and idle time equals the session; a hand-worked three-patient case; the same seed gives the same result). Consultation times are modelled as lognormal rather than gamma because of a lower AIC ({{ q('sim_service_time_fit', {'family': 'lognormal'}, 'aic', 'aic') }} against {{ q('sim_service_time_fit', {'family': 'gamma'}, 'aic', 'aic') }}); the QQ plot shows the lognormal following the data closely, with a slightly heavier tail in the data than the fit at the top end. Hangu's median consultation of {{ q('sim_service_time_fit', {'family': 'lognormal'}, 'median_service_min', 1) }} minutes gives a default slot of {{ q('sim_service_time_fit', {'family': 'lognormal'}, 'slot_minutes_default', 0) }} minutes (the median rounded up to the next five) and a session of {{ q('sim_service_time_fit', {'family': 'lognormal'}, 'session_minutes_default', 0) }} minutes, which is {{ q('s2_policy_tradeoffs', {'policy': 'P0'}, 'n_slots', 0) }} slots. The file has no consultation shorter than {{ lit('3') }} minutes, so its short end is missing.
{% set _ = expect(v('test_summary', {'group': 'module', 'name': 'tests/test_sim.py'}, 'failed') == 0 and v('test_summary', {'group': 'module', 'name': 'tests/test_sim_experiments.py'}, 'failed') == 0, 'the simulation tests pass') %}
{% set _ = expect(v('sim_service_time_fit', {'family': 'lognormal'}, 'aic') < v('sim_service_time_fit', {'family': 'gamma'}, 'aic'), 'the lognormal has the lower AIC') %}

Report chapter: Chapter 7 (Implementation and Testing) and Chapter 8 (Results, Table 20, Figures 17 and 18).

### S2 Policy trade-offs

Every setting uses {{ q('s2_policy_tradeoffs', {'policy': 'P0'}, 'n_reps', 'int') }} replications, with seed {{ q('s2_policy_tradeoffs', {'policy': 'P0'}, 'base_seed', 0) }} plus the replication number. With no overbooking (P0) a session serves {{ s2('P0', 'patients_served', 2) }} patients on average, with a mean wait of {{ s2('P0', 'mean_wait_min') }} minutes, doctor overtime of {{ s2('P0', 'overtime_min') }} minutes and doctor idle time of {{ s2('P0', 'idle_min') }} minutes. Doubling every second slot (P1, k = 2) serves {{ s2('P1 k=2', 'patients_served', 2) }} patients but the wait becomes {{ s2('P1 k=2', 'mean_wait_min') }} minutes, overtime {{ s2('P1 k=2', 'overtime_min') }} minutes and idle time falls to {{ s2('P1 k=2', 'idle_min') }} minutes. A risk threshold at p90 (P2) double-books {{ s2('P2 p90', 'double_slots_mean', 2) }} slots per session and serves {{ s2('P2 p90', 'patients_served', 2) }} patients with a wait of {{ s2('P2 p90', 'mean_wait_min') }} minutes and overtime of {{ s2('P2 p90', 'overtime_min') }} minutes. Every extra patient served costs waiting and overtime, and no policy is best on all of them, so the report gives the trade-off table and does not combine it into one score: any weighting would be a choice made by the reader.

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Table 15, Figure 11).

### S3 If the predicted risk is wrong

A constant error in every predicted risk changes how many slots a fixed threshold double-books. For the P2 threshold of {{ lit('0.3') }}, unshifted, {{ s3('0.0', 'P2 0.3', 'double_slots_mean', 2) }} slots are double-booked and the mean wait is {{ s3('0.0', 'P2 0.3', 'mean_wait_min') }} minutes. Overestimating the risk by {{ lit('0.10') }} raises that to {{ s3('0.1', 'P2 0.3', 'double_slots_mean', 2) }} slots, a wait of {{ s3('0.1', 'P2 0.3', 'mean_wait_min') }} minutes and overtime of {{ s3('0.1', 'P2 0.3', 'overtime_min') }} minutes. Underestimating it by {{ lit('0.10') }} cuts it to {{ s3('-0.1', 'P2 0.3', 'double_slots_mean', 2) }} slots, a wait of {{ s3('-0.1', 'P2 0.3', 'mean_wait_min') }} minutes and {{ s3('-0.1', 'P2 0.3', 'patients_served', 2) }} patients served, barely more than the {{ s2('P0', 'patients_served', 2) }} of P0. The effect is lopsided: overestimating adds more waiting than underestimating removes, which is in line with the arXiv preprint by Amalina and An ({{ lit('2026') }}; not peer reviewed), though that comparison is of direction only.
{% set _ = expect(v('s3_probability_error', {'risk_shift': '0.1', 'policy': 'P2 0.3'}, 'mean_wait_min') - v('s3_probability_error', {'risk_shift': '0.0', 'policy': 'P2 0.3'}, 'mean_wait_min') > v('s3_probability_error', {'risk_shift': '0.0', 'policy': 'P2 0.3'}, 'mean_wait_min') - v('s3_probability_error', {'risk_shift': '-0.1', 'policy': 'P2 0.3'}, 'mean_wait_min'), 'overestimation hurts more than underestimation helps') %}

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Table 16, Figure 12).

### S4 If the clinic's no-show rate is different

Scaling every patient's no-show probability by one half gives a base rate of {{ s4('0.5', 'P0', 'base_no_show_rate', 'pct') }}, and by one and a half {{ s4('1.5', 'P0', 'base_no_show_rate', 'pct') }}. Without overbooking a session then serves {{ s4('0.5', 'P0', 'patients_served', 2) }} patients at the low rate and {{ s4('1.5', 'P0', 'patients_served', 2) }} at the high rate. Doubling every second slot costs a wait of {{ s4('0.5', 'P1 k=2', 'mean_wait_min') }} minutes at the low rate (patients come more often) and {{ s4('1.5', 'P1 k=2', 'mean_wait_min') }} minutes at the high rate. The fixed risk threshold of {{ lit('0.3') }} adapts: it double-books {{ s4('0.5', 'P2 0.3', 'double_slots_mean', 2) }} slots per session at the low rate and {{ s4('1.5', 'P2 0.3', 'double_slots_mean', 2) }} at the high rate, whereas the uniform and percentile policies double-book the same number whatever the rate. A policy that does not look at risk has to be re-tuned for each clinic.
{% set _ = expect(v('s4_base_rate', {'base_rate_scale': '0.5', 'policy': 'P2 0.3'}, 'double_slots_mean') < v('s4_base_rate', {'base_rate_scale': '1.5', 'policy': 'P2 0.3'}, 'double_slots_mean'), 'the fixed threshold doubles more slots when the base rate is higher') %}

Report chapter: Chapter 8 (Results, Table 17, Figure 13).

### S5 If consultation times vary more

With the mean consultation time held at {{ q('s5_service_variability', {'variability_scale': '1.0', 'policy': 'P0'}, 'service_mean_min', 1) }} minutes, the standard deviation is scaled from {{ q('s5_service_variability', {'variability_scale': '0.5', 'policy': 'P0'}, 'service_sd_min', 1) }} to {{ q('s5_service_variability', {'variability_scale': '1.0', 'policy': 'P0'}, 'service_sd_min', 1) }} to {{ q('s5_service_variability', {'variability_scale': '1.5', 'policy': 'P0'}, 'service_sd_min', 1) }} minutes. The same patients attend in every setting, so patients served do not change, but waiting does: with no overbooking the mean wait is {{ s5('0.5', 'P0', 'mean_wait_min', 2) }}, {{ s5('1.0', 'P0', 'mean_wait_min', 2) }} and {{ s5('1.5', 'P0', 'mean_wait_min', 2) }} minutes, and with every third slot doubled (P1, k = 3) it is {{ s5('0.5', 'P1 k=3', 'mean_wait_min') }}, {{ s5('1.0', 'P1 k=3', 'mean_wait_min') }} and {{ s5('1.5', 'P1 k=3', 'mean_wait_min') }} minutes. The more variable the consultations, the more overbooking costs.
