### S6 The value of prediction at equal overbooking (the headline)

More double-booking always serves more patients, so the fair question is whether choosing the slots by predicted risk beats choosing them evenly or at random when the number of double-booked slots is the same. In this experiment each comparison double-books exactly as many slots as the policy it is matched to, so patients served are identical across the ways of choosing (about {{ q('s6_value_of_prediction', {'scheme': 'matched_to_p1', 'setting': 'k=2', 'selector': 'risk_top_m'}, 'served_gain_per_double_slot', 2) }} extra patients per double-booked slot) and only waiting, overtime and collisions can differ. The P2 settings are named by the percentile of predicted risk used as the cut: p50 is a low cut that double-books many slots, p95 a high cut that double-books few. Differences below are paired over replications, in minutes, and below zero favours the risk-based choice.

Against evenly spaced slots, the risk threshold lowers the mean wait by a small amount in most settings: {{ s6ci('matched_to_p2', '0.3', 'p2_risk_threshold', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }} at the {{ lit('0.3') }} threshold, {{ s6ci('matched_to_p2', 'p70', 'p2_risk_threshold', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }} at p70, {{ s6ci('matched_to_p2', 'p80', 'p2_risk_threshold', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }} at p80, {{ s6ci('matched_to_p2', 'p90', 'p2_risk_threshold', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }} at p90 and {{ s6ci('matched_to_p2', 'p95', 'p2_risk_threshold', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }} at p95. At p50 the interval includes zero ({{ s6ci('matched_to_p2', 'p50', 'p2_risk_threshold', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }}). The overtime differences against evenly spaced slots are mostly inconclusive, and at p95 the risk threshold is slightly worse ({{ s6ci('matched_to_p2', 'p95', 'p2_risk_threshold', 'overtime_min_vs_uniform') }}). Against random slots the gain is larger, for example {{ s6ci('matched_to_p2', 'p50', 'p2_risk_threshold', 'mean_wait_min_vs_random') }} at p50.

The share of double-booked slots in which both patients attend, the collision that overbooking tries to avoid, is {{ q('s6_value_of_prediction', {'scheme': 'matched_to_p2', 'setting': '0.3', 'selector': 'p2_risk_threshold'}, 'share_double_slots_both_attend', 'pct') }} with the risk threshold at {{ lit('0.3') }}, against {{ q('s6_value_of_prediction', {'scheme': 'matched_to_p2', 'setting': '0.3', 'selector': 'uniform'}, 'share_double_slots_both_attend', 'pct') }} with evenly spaced slots, {{ q('s6_value_of_prediction', {'scheme': 'matched_to_p2', 'setting': '0.3', 'selector': 'random'}, 'share_double_slots_both_attend', 'pct') }} with random slots and {{ q('s6_value_of_prediction', {'scheme': 'matched_to_p2', 'setting': '0.3', 'selector': 'oracle'}, 'share_double_slots_both_attend', 'pct') }} for an oracle that knows who will not come. An oracle would do far better on waiting too ({{ s6ci('matched_to_p2', 'p80', 'oracle', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }} at p80), so the model captures only a small part of what perfect knowledge of attendance would give.

Matched to the uniform policies instead (the highest-risk slots against every k-th slot), the picture is mixed. The wait falls at k = 3, by {{ s6ci('matched_to_p1', 'k=3', 'risk_top_m', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }}, and at k = 6, by {{ s6ci('matched_to_p1', 'k=6', 'risk_top_m', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }}, but not clearly at k = 2 ({{ s6ci('matched_to_p1', 'k=2', 'risk_top_m', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }}) or k = 4 ({{ s6ci('matched_to_p1', 'k=4', 'risk_top_m', 'mean_wait_min_vs_uniform') }}), and the overtime at k = 6 is higher ({{ s6ci('matched_to_p1', 'k=6', 'risk_top_m', 'overtime_min_vs_uniform') }}).

In short, prediction adds a modest, not uniform, benefit over spacing the extra patients evenly, and a clearer one over picking at random. This is also the best case for the model, because in the simulation the true no-show risk is the model's own risk.
